{"tool_name":"lipop","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"lipop","home":"http://cbs.dtu.dk/services/LipoP","license":"Other","summary":"Predictions of lipoproteins and discrimination between lipoprotein signal peptides, other signal peptides and n-terminal membrane helices in Gram-negative bacteria.","addition_date":"2015-06-29T10:17:14Z","last_update_date":"2018-12-16T13:02:09Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Web application","Web service"],"version":["1.0"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:lipop","cost":"Free of charge (with restrictions)","credit":[{"email":"soren.brunak@cpr.ku.dk","name":"S\u00f8ren Brunak","orcidid":"http://orcid.org/0000-0003-0316-5866","typeEntity":"Person"},{"email":"ag@cbs.dtu.dk","name":"Agnieszka S. Juncker","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Primary contact"]},{"name":"CBS","typeEntity":"Institute","typeRole":["Provider"]}],"documentation":[{"type":["General"],"url":"http://www.cbs.dtu.dk/services/LipoP/LipoP1.0server.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2044"},"format":[{"term":"FASTA","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_1929"}]}],"note":"predictions of lipoproteins and discrimination between signal peptides","operation":[{"term":"Protein signal peptide detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0418"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Sequence report","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2955"},"format":[{"term":"GFF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_2305"}]}]}],"maturity":"Emerging","name":"LipoP","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"CBS","publication":[{"doi":"10.1110/ps.0303703","metadata":{"abstract":"A method to predict lipoprotein signal peptides in Gram-negative Eubacteria, LipoP, has been developed. The hidden Markov model (HMM) was able to distinguish between lipoproteins (SPaseII-cleaved proteins), SPaseI-cleaved proteins, cytoplasmic proteins, and transmembrane proteins. This predictor was able to predict 96.8% of the lipoproteins correctly with only 0.3% false positives in a set of SPaseI-cleaved, cytoplasmic, and transmembrane proteins. The results obtained were significantly better than those of previously developed methods. Even though Gram-positive lipoprotein signal peptides differ from Gram-negatives, the HMM was able to identify 92.9% of the lipoproteins included in a Gram-positive test set. A genome search was carried out for 12 Gram-negative genomes and one Gram-positive genome. The results for Escherichia coli K12 were compared with new experimental data, and the predictions by the HMM agree well with the experimentally verified lipoproteins. A neural network-based predictor was developed for comparison, and it gave very similar results.","authors":[{"name":"Brunak S."},{"name":"Juncker A.S."},{"name":"Krogh A."},{"name":"Nielsen H."},{"name":"Von Heijne G."},{"name":"Willenbrock H."}],"citationCount":815,"date":"2003-08-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Protein Science","title":"Prediction of lipoprotein signal peptides in Gram-negative bacteria"},"pmcid":"PMC2323952","pmid":"12876315","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Membrane and lipoproteins","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0820"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"LipoP","home":"https://bio.tools/lipop","license":"Other","version":"1.0","summary":"Predictions of lipoproteins and discrimination between lipoprotein signal peptides, other signal peptides and n-terminal membrane helices in Gram-negative bacteria.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:lipop:1.0/cmd/cbs.dtu.dk","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"general":true},"license":{"open_source":false,"osi":false},"publications":[{"entries":[{"ci